<title>ISRAEL: Israel approves investment pacts with nine states.</title>
<headline>Israel approves investment pacts with nine states.</headline>
<dateline>TEL AVIV 1997-03-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Israeli government has approved bilateral agreements with nine countries to protect foreign investment, Israel's Treasury said on Sunday.</p>
<p>The agreements, which define what constitutes foreign investment and set out a legal framework to settle claims, are with Uzbekistan, Albania, Ukraine, Argentina, Georgia, India, Turkey, Turkmenistan and Kazakhstan.</p>
<p>The ministry said the agreements were aimed at encouraging Israeli investment into developing countries, particularly those of the former Soviet Union and Latin America.</p>
<p>Avraham Talmor, the Treasury official who headed the team that negotiated the agreements, added that the accord would also foster joint investments in third countries.</p>
<p>Israel already has similar agreements with 20 other developing countries, he said, and was in negotiations with about 20 others.</p>
<p>-- Tel Aviv newsroom, 972-3-537-2211</p>
</text>
